     "statement_latex": "Proposition 6.2 Let $\\nu\\delta t=\\tau$ then\n\\[\\operatorname{l.i.m.}_{\\nu\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}[(W((k+1)\\delta t)-W(k\\delta t))^2]=\\tau,\\]\nwhere, for a sequence of random variables, $A_\\nu$ and a random variable $B$, we have\n\\[\\operatorname{l.i.m.}_{\\nu\\to\\infty}A_\\nu=B\\iff\\lim_{\\nu\\to\\infty}\\mathbb E(A_\\nu-B)^2=0.\\]\n(This is referred to as convergence in the mean square sense.)",
-    "proof_latex": "With $\\tau,\\nu$ fixed, we have $\\delta t=\\tau/\\nu$.\nLet $\\Delta W_{\\nu,k}=W((k+1)\\delta t)-W(k\\delta t)=W((k+1)\\tau/\\nu)-W(k\\tau/\\nu)$ and observe that\n\\[\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}\\Delta W_{\\nu,k}^2-\\tau=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}[\\Delta W_{\\nu,k}^2-\\delta t].\\]\nThen\n\\[I_\\nu\\stackrel{\\rm def}=\\mathbb E(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}\\Delta W_{\\nu,k}^2-\\tau)^2=\\mathbb E(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}[\\Delta W_{\\nu,k}^2-\\delta t])^2.\\]\nTerms of the form $Z_{\\nu,k}\\stackrel{\\rm def}=\\Delta W_{\\nu,k}^2-\\delta t$ are independent and have mean zero. Therefore\n\\[\\mathbb E(\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}Z_{\\nu,k})^2=\\mathbb E\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}Z_{\\nu,k}^2-2\\mathbb E\\sum_{k\\ne l}Z_{\\nu,k}Z_{\\nu,l}=\\mathbb E\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}Z_{\\nu,k}^2,\\]\nwhere the last equation follows from independence of the increments ($\\mathbb E Z_kZ_l=0$ if $k\\ne l$).\nAlso observe that $\\Delta W_k$ is a normally distributed random variable with the following properties\n\\[\\mathbb E\\Delta W_k=0;\\quad\\mathbb E\\Delta W_k^2=\\delta t=\\tau/\\nu;\\quad\\mathbb E\\Delta W_k^4=3\\delta t^2=3(\\tau/\\nu)^2.\\]\nThus we have\n\\[I_\\nu=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}\\mathbb E(\\Delta \\Delta W_{\\nu,k}^2-\\delta t)^2\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}\\mathbb E[\\Delta \\Delta W_{\\nu,k}^4-2\\delta t\\Delta \\Delta W_{\\nu,k}^2+\\delta t^2]\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\nu-1}[3\\delta t^2-2\\delta t^2+\\delta t^2]=2\\nu\\delta t^2=2\\tau^2/\\nu.\\]\nThus $I_\\nu\\to0$ as $\\nu\\to0$, which is the desired result.",
